(a) No person shall permit, allow, or use property located in any residential district in the City for any commercial parking purpose, except as hereinafter provided.
(b) The parking of commercial vehicles in residential districts shall be located wholly within a garage or carport.
(c) For purposes of this section, "commercial vehicle" includes:
(1) Any vehicle having a gross vehicle weight rating in excess of eight thousand (8,000) pounds. "Gross vehicle weight rating" (GVWR) means the value specified by the manufacturer or manufacturers as the maximum loaded weight of a single or a combination (articulated) vehicle, or registered gross weight, whichever is greater. The gross vehicle weight rating of a combination (articulated) vehicle, commonly referred to as the "gross combination weight rating" (GCWR), is the gross vehicle weight rating of the power unit plus the gross vehicle weight rating of the towed unit or units. In the absence of a value specified for the towed unit or units by the manufacturer or manufacturers, the gross vehicle weight rating of a combination (articulated) vehicle is the gross vehicle weight rating of the power unit plus the total weight of the towed unit or units, including the loads on them;
(2) Any vehicle greater than eight (8) feet in height;
(3) Any vehicle greater than twenty-three (23) feet in length;
(4) Any vehicle designated to transport more than eight (8) passengers.
"Commercial vehicle" specially includes unattached trailers or flat beds designed to be pulled by a vehicle or cab meeting the definition of commercial vehicle.
(d) The restrictions on parking commercial vehicles in residential districts contained in this section shall not apply in the following circumstances:
(1) While any such vehicle is expeditiously loading or unloading passengers, freight, materials or merchandise between the hours of 7:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m.;
(2) While the owners or operators of any such vehicle or equipment are engaged in the performance of a service on the property or on property in the immediate vicinity for a period of time reasonably necessary to complete the service;
(3) While effecting emergency repairs when a commercial vehicle is so disabled as to render it unsafe to proceed further;
(4) Commercial vehicles engaged in street construction, maintenance or repair of public service utilities;
(5) Emergency vehicles including, but not limited to, law enforcement vehicles, fire department vehicles, ambulances, and vehicles operated by emergency medical services; and
(6) No more than one (1) commercial vehicle licensed for home occupation use.
(e) This section shall not apply to church, school, nursing home, or retirement home vehicles parked upon property owned by such institutions in residential districts.
(f) This section shall not be applicable to recreational vehicles and equipment, the parking of which is regulated by Section 608 of the zoning ordinance.
(g) This section 19-63 shall not apply to vehicles which are designated as handicap vehicles or to conversion vans.
(h) The provisions of this section shall apply to the following persons: (i) the owner of any such commercial vehicle; (ii) the driver of any such commercial vehicle; and (iii) any person who owns or occupies private property located in a residential district upon which such commercial vehicle is located in violation of this section.
